  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This eLife article is an early but already visible contribution to the study of GPCR evolution in the closest living relatives of animals. eLife judged the work \u201cImportant\u201d with \u201cCompelling\u201d evidence, and the record shows one scholarly citation so far, alongside documented downstream reuse of the article\u2019s data and materials.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Garcia De Las Bayonas and King tackle a longstanding gap in evolutionary biology: how G-protein-coupled receptors and related signaling components diversified in choanoflagellates and other close relatives of metazoans. The article\u2019s eLife assessment describes the study as important and the evidence as compelling, highlighting the breadth of new genomic data brought to bear on GPCRs, G proteins, GRKs, and RIC8 proteins. That editorial judgment suggests the paper was viewed as a substantive contribution to a technically demanding area.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The uptake record is still modest in volume, but it is not empty. OpenAlex shows one citation, appearing in a 2026 Bioscience Reports article on purinergic signaling. The citing paper\u2019s concepts point to a neighboring but distinct area of receptor biology and neurophysiology, which is consistent with a broad foundational role for this work rather than immediate concentration in a single subfield.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citation, the article shows signs of reuse. The stewardship record lists multiple Figshare datasets associated with the work, and the reuse graph indicates two documented downstream reuse events, including a Biomedical Article Mention in Europe PMC. This suggests that the paper\u2019s outputs are being handled as reusable research assets, even though the citation trail is still early.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Accessibility likely supports that trajectory. The article is gold open access and is available through multiple open instances in repository records, which reduces barriers to reading and reuse. The authors also acknowledge Human Frontier Science Program support, underscoring that the work sits within a funded research program rather than emerging as an isolated effort.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, this is a clearly positioned foundational paper with early evidence of scholarly reach. Its strongest signals are expert editorial endorsement, open dissemination, and measurable reuse of associated research outputs; the citation record is just beginning to form, so the most defensible reading is that influence is emerging rather than yet widely diffused.</p>",
